# srcbuild compiles a pug file that lives outside `src/pug` into `.view/.@root/`,
# at a path mirroring the source's own location under the project root. Templates
# deployed at runtime land there too ( `.@root/user/template/...` ), so this
# subtree holds user data, and it is the one part of `.view/` that genuinely has
# to be generated at request time. Never committed, unlike the rest of `.view/`
# in an app that commits its build artifacts - see the note at the bottom.
**/.view/.@root/
package-lock.json
.version
module/version/index.pug
config/**/local.*
dev/srcbuild/static/
.bundle-dep
context/shared

# --- app-level overrides (downstream leaf apps) ---
# Append-only, so everything above stays byte-identical across the base and every
# app built on it and merges cleanly. servebase (the base) ignores package-lock.json;
# a deployable leaf app should re-include and commit its lockfiles here for
# reproducible `npm ci` installs by adding:  !package-lock.json
# (node_modules/**/package-lock.json stays ignored: its parent dir is excluded.)
#
# An app that re-includes its own frontend (e.g. !frontend/web) thereby commits
# that frontend's `static/`, `manifest.json` AND `.view/`, and for `.view/` that
# is deliberate rather than an oversight: precompiled templates inline whatever
# they `include`, `@/...` ones out of node_modules included, while the view
# engine's staleness check only compares that one .pug's own mtime and never
# notices an include changing underneath it. Committing `.view/` alongside the
# lockfile is what keeps the two in step - build against an `npm ci` tree and
# what is deployed matches what it was compiled against. `.@root/` is excluded
# above and stays runtime state.
